Zeiss Ikon was predominantly East German.  The Zeiss cameras were far more 
popular with Europeans (and Japanese!) than with Americans, who couldn't get 
the good models without buying abroad and smuggling them home, or buying the 
cheaper Western Germany Zeiss branded cameras.  Much like with Cuban cigars 
or rum today.

Americans typically used either Kodak Eastman or Japanese cameras rebranded 
to US names (like Honeywell and Sears), and later on, after Japanese became 
kosher, they didn't mind carrying around a Nikon or Canon.  Until the 
arrival of Polaroid and Kodak Instamatic cameras in the early seventies --  
the archetypical American tourist would use them, being perceived as more 
concerned with ease of use or instant gratification than picture quality.

The typical British tourist, on the other hand, would use an old 120 mm 
accordion style camera handed down from his father, and carried around a 30 
pound tripod to attach it to.  No picture was taken without first arranging 
everyone to the complete satisfaction of the camera owner, a quite lengthy 
process.  Those old cameras took surprisingly good pictures, though.

